{
  "abstract": "Token conservation does not ensure that a finite pretraining run exposes every source document. We analyze a streaming packing family that maintains a minimum buffer count, fits whole spans when possible, and otherwise selects the shortest span. An exact invariant shows that its largest B−1 original over-context source occurrences remain buffered and unstarted, even when selected suffixes are preserved losslessly. For a repeated finite corpus without buffer reset, a unique longest source cannot begin before its B-th arrival. In the supplied 50.3-million-parameter pretraining recipe, an exact 499.65-million-target audit finds 27.24 million source tokens protected in 999 untouched documents; lossless continuation still protects 25.05 million. The complete eligible corpus implies necessary first-service bounds reaching 1000 source passes for its unique maximum. Six matched training runs compare the original loader with source-matched random spans and conventional segmentation at two seeds. Both interventions worsen the original held-out metric, but frozen source-disjoint confirmation supports better tail-window prediction relative to beginnings in all eight predeclared method/seed/length contrasts, with adjusted source-bootstrap intervals excluding zero. Development position decomposition shows the improvement is not confined to immediate BOS-reset predictions. Seven checkpoints show no ranking reversals on prefixes of the same original evaluation population. We contribute a precise inclusion theorem, independent checker and reproducible population audit, not a new packing algorithm or general quality improvement. Limits include one model/corpus, two model seeds and artificially reset conditional windows.",
